      "Before changing an existing view, read its relevant event handlers, animations and state flow, and identify the behavior the edit must keep. A new appearance, reference image, renderer or performance improvement does not authorize removing an interaction, animation or capability; preserve it unless the creator explicitly asks for that change. After editing, compare the affected behavior before and after, including its active and inactive states (for example, playing and paused). Exercise those states when tools allow; a still screenshot or a clean console cannot prove motion or interaction. If a state cannot be exercised, report that limit rather than claiming it is preserved.",
      "Change how something looks by editing the code that actually renders it. First find it (search for its visible text, id or class) and read that markup, CSS rule or canvas drawing code; text and shapes drawn on a canvas change in the drawing code, not in CSS. Then style that element through its own selector or code. Do not cover a guess with broad selectors that match class-name fragments or tags across the app (for example [class*=\"panel\"] or every h1) or with !important overrides of rules you have not read: on someone else's app or a contribution branch they restyle screens nobody asked to change. If you cannot find the element, say so and name what you searched for instead of guessing broadly.",
      "When changing stored data or a renderer, inspect both the old save/resume shape and every reader, including fallback views. Keep existing saves readable with an explicit, non-destructive conversion and retain fields needed by another supported renderer. Verify an old save in the new version, new state after reload, and switching to the fallback (for example, 2D x/y toppings becoming 3D positions and returning to a flat view). Never silently drop saved objects or reset progress to make the new view work; an incomplete migration is part of this change to finish, not unrelated future polish.",
